In article <36AEF8B1.CFC5CFCA@ev.co.yu>, Nikola Milutinovic <Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu> writes:

...  Nikola Milutinovic and Waco Johnny D arguing over the right
way to rebuild a Digital UNIX or DEC OSF/1 kernel to add a tape.

You're both still wrong.  You DO NOT need to rebuild kernel to add
a disk or tape to an existing SCSI adapter.  Please stop spreading
misinformation.

The SCSI CAM subsystem has always been able to detect new devices
on existing busses by a forced scan with scu(8).  Once a device is
found, the special files can be created with /dev/MAKEDEV or on
newer versions scsimgr.  On versions with scsimgr it should create
the necessary files when the system boots.

If the SCSI disk is connected through one of the backplane array
controllers, KZESC, KZPSC or KZPAC, you may need to rebuild the
kernel.  But these aren't SCSI controllers and have their own
driver.

The MSCP driver may need a configuration file change.  However,
the clever system manager will add lines for all the likely
devices in advance and only need to make changes for unusual
devices.

--
Alan Rollow				alan@nabeth.cxo.dec.com




