Organization: Silver Star Ranch
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

As with most discussions in the computing world, the answer is, "It depends."
My 4GB 4100 servers run with 128MB of swap from the b partition of one disk.
I know the systems are stable and I don't want space for a dump.  I know the VM
consumption of the systems at full load, and I've used vmubc and collect to
watch free memory over time, so I know my chances of swapping are zilch.

I think the need for swap space:

  - decreases with a system's "transaction-based nature," swapping being the
bane of a responsive interactive system
  - increases with uncertainty about system VM usage under full load
  - increases with uncertainty regarding system (application and OS) stability
and therefore regarding the requirement to capture dumps early in the system's
life
  - increases with incomplete/ignored UBC tuning
  - increases with a system's "newness in the shop" due to the production
admins not having a feel for it yet

All that said, disks are getting cheap enough so that having the swap space
there isn't that much of a financial hardship.  Well, that is, until the point
where you have to buy a new controller and SCSI shelf to put the swap disk in
because all your current ones are full!  ;-)

I agree with you -- those "blanket statements" are a little hard to take.

WJD





From -5472872748867814187
X-Google-Language: ENGLISH,ASCII-7-bit
X-Google-Thread: fee84,8d75d59151f4fbdb
X-Google-Attributes: gidfee84,public
From: Waco Johnny D <WJD@nowhere.com>
Subject: Re: tape drive
Date: 1999/01/26
Message-ID: <36AE1223.EF4E8849@nowhere.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 437214178
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 7bit
References: <36AE09CF.41C6@chemdept.chem.ou.edu>
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=us-ascii
Organization: Silver Star Ranch
Mime-Version: 1.0
Newsgroups: comp.unix.osf.osf1

Connect the tape drive first.

Then at the console prompt (>>>) do a SHOW DEV to see if the thing shows
up.

Then in OSF:

cp /vmunix /vmunix.old  (Saves the old kernel)
cp /genvmunix /vmunix  (Copies the generic kernel to the one booted by
default)
shutdown -r now  (Reboot the system; this will boot the generic kernel)
doconfig -c /sys/conf/<uppercase_systemname>  (or just "doconfig"
without any switches  if you don't want to use the kernel configuration
file)
Do you want to replace the kernel? YES.
cp /sys/<uppercase_systemname>/vmunix /vmunix  (Makes the kernel you
just built the
one booted by default -- this new kernel name will be identified as such
near the bottom of your screen after "doconfig" is done)
shutdown -r now (reboot the system into the brand-new kernel which
hopefully now
contains the device special files for your tape drive)

Once the system comes back up, the following command should spit out the

device-special files for the tape drive:

file /dev/*rmt*

N.B. there are other ways to do this such as making the device-special
files
manually or just doing a doconfig w/o all the baloney around saving the
old kernel and
automatically building a new one out of a generic, but I typically use
the above method when adding new devices and it's rarely failed me.  I
admit it is a little conservative.

Take a look at the man page for "doconfig" for more info.

WJD





