Remco Wouts <remco@xray.bmc.uu.se> writes:

>We have a mixed network of machines running linux, osf1, and irix. All
>machines serve disks to each other over NFS. There seems to be a huge
>performance problem when writing on linux to disks mounted from our
>ALPHA'S, whereas writing to the SGI's works as advertised. I did a
>dejanews search and a few people mention the same problem but no
>solution has been posted. Usually linux get's the blame. Since
>everything works fine with IRIX I really think there is something
>funny with the NFS2 implementation under OSF1.

Technically, there's something funny with SGI.  And something really
ancient with Linux.

What you're seeing is a protocol-compliant Digital UNIX system fed by
a Linux client that isn't using a basic NFS acceleration technique that
Digital UNIX expects.

A "proper" NFS V2 NFS server doesn't return a write reply until all data
is securely on disk.  That way, if you write foo.c, and the
server loses power, the data will still be on the server when it comes
back.  In practice, this means something like three disk writes
per NFS write, usually with a seek in between:

1) Data

2) Indirect block

3) Inode (where file size and other metadata lives).

Most NFS clients, run a program called biod or nfsiod that provides
additional threads that take of the task of sheparding writes to
completion.  Digital UNIX takes that a step further and _expects_
clients to be doing that (look at nfsiod(8) on Digital UNIX for more
details and notes on read ahead).

Digital UNIX servers use a technique called "write gathering" to
greatly speed up NFS V2 writes.  When the first write comes in the
server releases the data to the disk, but doesn't reply.  It sleeps
for a bit to allow more requests to come in.  Those just send the data
to the disk and add don't reply. Eventually the first wakes up, looks
around for more requests that haven't been processed yet.  Ultimately,
some server thread concludes no more are likely to show up, commits
all the data and metadata, and returns replies for all the writes.  We
try to tune the system to gather 8 writes (64KB) at a time.  Three
seeks, 3-10 disk writes (we gather disk writes too!) - a lot better
than a simple server.

I've only recently received confirmation that Linux isn't using biod, at
least not by default.  If you can find biod, run it!

Worse, Linux is doing a mere 1 KB write.  Just about everyone else (well,
discounting NT), sends 8 KB or more.  If Linux sent 8 KB, its throughput
would be about 8X faster.  See if you can do that.

The worst thing, at least in my mind, is that Linux is using NFS V2.  We
fixed the NFS write performance problem in NFS V3, which has shipped on
Digital UNIX since 1994.  What you show here is about state of the art for
1985.  Why, oh why hasn't anyone ported FreeBSD's NFS to Linux yet?  V3,
biods, TCP, a server in the kernel, all sorts of great stuff.

As for SGI, you're using something called asynchronous mode where the
server replies to the writes but hasn't committed your data to disk.
Even if you call fsync(2) or sync(2) on the client, your data won't
be written.  Digital has never supported that mode, relying on write
gathering or Presto support (battery-backed cache RAM) for V2 performance.
With the advent of NFS V3 there's little impetus to enhance/corrupt
NFS V2.

Your simplest solution is to buy a Presto board for the Digital UNIX
server, but I'd be delighted if you brought Linux into the 1990s.

Somewhat of a workaround is to patch the Digital UNIX server with dbx.
Change variable nfs_ufs_lbolt from 1 to 0 and it will skip that sleep
before looking for other in progress NFS writes.  That should save 8
msec per write request.  Hmm.  We should reduce that ourselves, we
sleep only 2 msec on writes received on FDDI, the same speed as your
100 Mbit Ethernet.

BTW, this is fairly recent news to us too.  I wanted to check RedHat
5.2, look for biod, and maybe find the Linux NFS community before addressing
this, but this timing is okay too.  :-)

