> Connect the tape drive first.
> 
> Then at the console prompt (>>>) do a SHOW DEV to see if the thing shows
> up.
> 
> Then in OSF:
> 
> cp /vmunix /vmunix.old  (Saves the old kernel)
> cp /genvmunix /vmunix  (Copies the generic kernel to the one booted by
> default)
> shutdown -r now  (Reboot the system; this will boot the generic kernel)

All this could have been done like this:

>>> boot -file genvmunix
# cp /vmunix /vmunix.old

> doconfig -c /sys/conf/<uppercase_systemname>  (or just "doconfig"
> without any switches  if you don't want to use the kernel configuration
> file)
> Do you want to replace the kernel? YES.

Not sure. Old versions of OSF/1 had all device disks listed in
/sys/conf/<SYSNAME>. If you leave that file, new devices won't appear. New DU
4.0 uses "Plug'n'Play". Just list the controller and all disks on it will be
recognized. Maybe there's a mechanism underneath, but I haven't seen it.

So, if old OSF is the OS, then he should run just "doconfig" and it will
re-create the configuration file. In the case of DU 4.0, he should just create
/dev/ files for the tape. (assuming 3 is the SCSI ID of the tape)

# cd /dev ; ./MAKEDEV tz3

Nix.
