In article <36AE09CF.41C6@chemdept.chem.ou.edu>, "Scott E. Boesch" <sboesch@chemdept.chem.ou.edu> writes:
>
>We have a Dec Alpha 4100 Server Running Digital Unix 4.0D.
>How do you define and/or configure an external tape drive to
>be recognized by the system?

If you have an existing SCSI controller that you can put the
drive on, simply pick a unique ID and add the drive to the bus.
If you have to unterminate the bus to add the drive, it would
be wise to shutdown.  When you reboot the operating system
will find the drive and create the necessary special files.

If you don't have to shutdown to add the drive, you can use
scu(8) to scan the bus for the device, verify its location
and then create the special files by hand:

# scu scan edt
# scu show edt | grep Seq

From this subset listing of the SCSI devices, figure which is
the device you added and then use the bus and target number
to construct the base device name:

	tz#, where # = (bus * 8) + target-id

# cd /dev
# ./MAKEDEV tz#

Please note, that you don't have to rebuild the kernel for an
existing SCSI controller.  If you're adding a new SCSI controller
for the drive, you will have to rebuild the kernel.

>or
>Does anyone know if it is possible to backup the system up
>over the network?

Many backup products support using a central system as a
backup with client side software to backup files.  Networker
is a good example.  For UFS you can use rdump to backup to
another system that understands the protocol.  For AdvFS
and vdump you have to write to stdout and pipe that to a
remote shell that runs something on the server system to
write to tape.

