What does "fails when trying to load the OS" entail guy?

Have you tried booting to single user mode to see if you can at least get
that far?  From the console prompt (>>>) where you normally type "b" or
"boot", enter "boot -s" (or "boot s" - I can never remember until I have to
do it 8)).  If it's not the root filesystem that's trashed you should be
able to get to single user mode (which basically means you are root without
having to login).  If you can get to that point, then check /etc/fstab to
see where the /usr filesystem exists and run an "fsck" against it if it is a
UFS filesystem or "verify" if it is AdVfs.

If, however, it is the root filesystem that's corrupted (or the bootblock),
then you really need the OS installation CD to boot from and work from
there.

All in all, I hate to tell you this but this could be very tricky and
dangerous and you should, if at all possible, get someone with more
experience to help or have your company sport the money for a purchase order
for a DEC support call.  This is really not the place to start learning UNIX
System Admin.  Also, get a copy of O'Reilly & Associates "Essential System
Administration" (or some other good book) pronto if you don't have the
system documentation.

I wish I could be of more help (like be there to help), but without seeing
where the boot is hanging I can't really point you to anything else.

Later,
Gene...


