Nikola Milutinovic wrote:
> > I always presumed it was to prevent UID reuse.
> 
> For what purpose?
> 

To keep from accidently giving the wrong access to the wrong files?

Personally, I have never run into it (at least not on my DUNIX
system)... But let's say you have a shared area where a user has created
some files. The person leaves and you truly delete the account,
forgetting about the shared file area. A new user, in a totally
different department, comes in and you use the "use next UID" option.
The new user is assigned the UID of the user that left. Since file
ownership is all UID based once you boil out the username, the new user
is now the owner of those files in that shared area that the previous
person had control over... Perhaps a salary listing (sounds like an IBM
commercial).

That may be one reason not to reuse UID's. Of course, relying on file
ownerships/UIDs for protection is not the best way of doing things, but
it could happen.
-- 

