In article <36A5D1FB.1DF8BB28@ev.co.yu>, Nikola Milutinovic
<Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu> wrote:

>jxfaultl@bbechtel.com wrote:
>> 
>> Nikola Milutinovic <Nikola.Milutinovic@ev.co.yu> wrote:
>> > The idea behind "retiring user" still eludes me. A retired user cannot
>> 
>> I always presumed it was to prevent UID reuse.
>
>For what purpose?

Old files owned by the previous owner would be inherited by the new user
using the old UID.  The new user may not be the correct person to view old
files.

Now old files can be found using various utilities and removed them or
change their ownership, but there are possible ACL's that make it more
difficult.  An ACL can contain either a username or a UID.  If there are
files that the old user was granted access to via ACL's, the new user may
not be someone you want to view those files via ACL's.  Finding all the
ACL's using the old UID may be more difficult.

Quotas are assigned by UID.  So cleaning up all the file systems where the
old UID may have had quota is annoying.

There may be other places the old UID was used that are over looked.

From an administrative point of view, it is easier to just use a new UID
and allow the old UID to remain unused until you run out of UIDs and need
to reuse older ones because you have no choice.

-- 
