> We are working with Digital Unix on a DEC WS 500au, and we use
> memory-intensive process (such as dictionaries with up to 14 MB). Now we
> need to change some system parameters because the dictionary process does
> not work as we want, after 120 MB of VM the process terminates with a
> memory-fault message.
> There's some way to modify the system to allow such process? Which
> parameters are involved? Do we have to recompile the kernel?
> 
> thanks in advance
Hi,
 I think your system is running out of available RAM and as well the
swap space available to be used for as VM. So the easiest would be to
add some swap space and try it out.

I had this problem of bringing on a large banking application which had
200 processes to be started at the beginning and the system would start
120 out of them and then cry and as most of them were using Dynamic
linking so I used to get some error like Not enough memory.

So I did the research for couple of days basing on the theory that Unix
does allow you to run a porcess in virtual memory. But finally I added
256 MB of swap sapce and it worked.

Try out.

