Hi!

claird@Starbase.NeoSoft.COM (Cameron Laird) writes:

> The misbehaving host reports itself as
> 
>   Digital UNIX V4.0B  (Rev. 564); Tue Oct 21 08:31:20 EDT 1997
> 
> unpatched.  We've have thoroughly happy results with 4.0A (and
> are still running at least one 3.2 node, incidentally).

I've seen something on a 4.0B system which matches part of your
description, so it might help:

On my last job we had a 2100 (4.0b patchkit 4 or 5) which lost network
connection from time to time with time between failures decreasing.  It
was connected to FDDI with a DAS-Controller and unplugging the active
link made the system regain network connection instantly.
I don't think it's FDDI-related, so just unplug your network for a
second and check if system looks ok again. If yes, it could be the same
problem.
You wrote that system load is almost 0.  On our system I noticed around
99.9% system time during those blackouts.  I enabled kernel profiling
(=> man kprofile) and noticed almost all the time was spend in a
function called 'tcp_reass'. The problem seemed to occur when the system
has a high number of network connections (our system was printserver for
~5000 network printers).  DEC supplied us with a patch for 4.0B and
things looked normal again.  Unfortunately I don't know, if this patch
made it into any of the newer patch kits.

Hope this helps

-- 
Stefan



