I'm currently trying to take over systems and network management within a
company offering training services. We have a mix of operating systems to
cover including MS Windows 95/98/NT, OpenVMS and Digital UNIX. Since most of
the courses are quite "destructive" we are looking for the ultimate way to
restore these systems after the course.

For the Windows products we are currently using a rather ugly bunch of
mechanisms based on unzip/unattended installs and cloning SW (Ghost), for
OpenVMS we are using an OVMS cluster with an image restore (thanks God for
OVMS clustering!) and for DUNIX we are now using a combination of DMS
(network boot) and restore/vrestore relying on heavily customized RIS
scripts.

As usual the previous system manager did not document any of the
installation methods so we are looking at a whole lot of reverse engineering
to find out what exactly these mechanisms try to do and how they are
supposed to do it.

I'm rather new to Digital UNIX and I was wondering if we should not build up
something from scratch relying on standard techniques (i.e. as little
customizations as possible) instead of trying to wade through all these
customized scripts trying to figure out what is going on. I would be
extremely grateful for any suggestions concerning fast disk recovery
techniques covering any of the OSses mentioned above. Concerning DUNIX I saw
some pointers concerning cloning in the installation manual. Since most
machines are identical (HW & SW) I was wondering if this is the way to go.

Meanwhile I try to figure out what this 3Com Oncore 5000 hub is all about!

Thanks a lot for your feedback!

Johan






