Tom Goessi wrote:
> 
> I entered the parameters of the new PCI card into this file,
> but the message still appears. I think there exists a configuration
> tool, which should do this entries from the /usr/sys/conf/.product.list or
> the /usr/sys/conf/<MACHINE>.list files, where the driver install utility
> entered the parameters from the driver to build the new kernel with
> doconfig.
> Does somebody know more  about this?

pci_option_data.c is a *strongly* deprecated way of adding a new
pci option to the system.  A driver should define a module name
using the system configuration tools.  This name then becomes a
module in the /etc/sysconfigtab file (managed by sysconfigdb and
sysconfig commands).  PCI card identification is performed by adding
data to a well-known name in this module.  Driver installation
should have included steps to install the .mod file someplace 
logical, 'kreg' the location of the file, perform 'sysconfigdb'
commands to add driver options and define supported pci cards, and
(optional if dynamically linking the driver to the kernel) rebuild
the kernel.

See the man pages for the above commands and the device driver
reference and tutorial for more details.


