John P Williams wrote:

> Also note that multi-threaded programs are supported by the tools you mention
> only if those programs are linked with libpthread; that is, if they use
> either the CMA or POSIX (pthread) interfaces to libpthread. Other
> implementations of pthreads are not supported by these tools.
>
> cdp2@engr.uark.edu wrote:
> >
> >   I've got a multi-threaded (pthreads not DEC threads)

Just to correct a few thread misconceptions in the question and reply:

  1. Only the POSIX interface existing in libpthread. The "legacy" interfaces
     (CMA and the two DCE thread interfaces) are in libpthreads (note the "s"),
     which lays "on top of" libpthread.
  2. "DECthreads" (not "DEC threads") is the name of the thread implementation
     common to Compaq's "Digital UNIX" and OpenVMS. DECthreads supports a number
     of interfaces. POSIX 1003.1c-1995 is the primary DECthreads interface, but
     CMA and the two DCE thread interfaces are also part of DECthreads.

Trivial distinctions, perhaps, but technical communication without common terms
is futile.

