Hans Westerback wrote:
> 
> Hi,
> 
> I've noticed that on some systems root has been changed to use korn
> shell. But earlier I have heard that root should always run Bourne
> shell. Is this something that has changed in later versions? Are there
> any problems with running korn shell? What's the official view?

Officially, you should use "the most system" shell for root and those
are /bin/sh and /usr/bin/sh. The first is statically linked. The idea is
that root is the system account which is used only for system
administration.

I use ZSh on all of my machines for root, because I like it (I've had
couple of problems with it, but was able to fix them). The main argument
against using "non-Bourne shell" for root is that it may get broken.
That is why, on Digital UNIX, when in single user mode, root always gets
Bourne shell, regardless of what /etc/passwd says about it.

So, use what you like. If it starts messing things up, do: /bin/sh -c
"YOUR COMMAND" and it should be still OK.

Nix.





