Unless you have an alternate definition of alias file location in your
sendmail.cf (option OA), there's something wierd going on. You could try
two things (alias@DEC.foo.com is an alias):

DEC# sendmail -bt
....
3,0 alias@DEC.foo.com
.....
.....

this should give you the complete address translation process, rule by
rule. It should come out to your alias beaing recognized as local
address.

DEC# telnet localhost smtp
220 Sendmail 5.5.6 .......
VRFY alias@DEC.foo.com
250 <alias@DEC.foo.com>		<-- This means OK

EXPN alias@DEC.foo.com
220-<john@DEC.foo.com>
220-<jim@DEC.foo.com>
220 <mike@DEC.foo.com>

Basically, the report code should give you a hint, it is always a three
digit number xyz, with the meaning:

x-main status, yz-"sub-status". Now, if I remember correctly

x	meaning
----------------------
1	<I don't remember>
2	OK
3	Transient OK
4	Transient Err
5	Final Err
6	ESMTP
7	ESMTP

What I do remember,

220 = OK, information follows
250 = OK, request completed OK
252 = OK, result follows
550 = Wrong address

Nix.




