In fact, most of the stuff in /sbin is not statically linked either.

libc.so resides in the root filesystem (in /shlib) so it's available whenver
the root filesystem is available, but there still is that chance that if
something horrible goes wrong with your shared C library (like during the
patch process) you may not even be able to get a working shell.

On Digital Unix init always starts the Bourne shell when in single user mode,
so I've been pretty comfortable setting root's shell to ksh (a good
compromise) and moving root's home directory to /root (on /, of course) on
all our DU machines.

Tim
