>Scripts in /sbin/rc?.d/ starting with letter 'S' are for entering
>this run level. Scripts starting with 'K' are for leaving it, so
>that if putting 'K' scripts in /sbin/rc2.d with execute them at
>the system shutdown rather then at the start up, and with the wrong
>argument ('stop' instead of 'start') to boot.
>
>Probably not that you wanted, yeah?

Look at them more closely... The 'S' and 'K' scripts are symlinked to
files in /sbin/init.d. If the sym-linked filename being with an 'S' at a
particular run-level, the script is executed with a 'start' parameter,
and if if begins with a 'K', the same script is executed with the 'stop'
parameter.

