The only requirement for any user to be able to SU root is that he needs to be
a member of the "system" group in /etc/group.

sudo works, and you can also call the encrypted version of root's password in a
shell script; I don't see any restrictions on which shell you use but I might
stick with Korn or Bourne as most Digital UNIX systems are installed with root
under the Bourne, Korn, or a weird thing called "Korn under the Bourne Shell."

