>>, ###neujdh@ttuhsc.edu### (Joe Hargis) writes:
>>
>>I have been backing up files with the vdump command, speciffically
>>"vdump -0 /" or "vdump -0 /usr", or whatever I wanted to dump.  No
>>problem.
>>
>>Now, I can dump one file system as above, but when I try to dump the
>>next one, I get the error message "vdump:  device </dev/rmt0h> is
>>OFFLINE".
>
>To the best of my knowledge (and I've never looked that closely)
>vdump has always taken the drive offline at the end of a backup
>if you don't use the no-rewind device.  Perhaps before V4.0D it
>would attempt to bring it online when starting a backup and that
>was changed in V4.0D.  It is a reasonable change, since it prevents
>vdump from overwriting a tape without explicit direction from the
>operator/system manager.
>
>>Anyone know what I'm doing wrong and how to fix it?
>

Hi, Joe:

That's easy:

...all you have to do is to include the "no-rewind" flag in your vdump
statement. Let's say you want to back up your root and usr domains to one tape
withour running into th rewind problem. The syntax would be (for a full vdump):

"vdump -0N root_domain && vdump -0N usr_domain",  where "N" is the "no-rewind"
flag and the "&&" says "if the root vdump completes succesfully, then proceed
with the usr_domain vdump."

Best Regards,

Mark Ray
Digital UNIX Systems Administrator
S & S Worldwide, Corp.



