Leon Muratov wrote:
> 
> Hello Newsgroup,
> Can somebody tell me what is the best way to increase swap space on DEC
> UNIX?
> On HP_SUX and AIX I could create a new partition and add the second swap
> space there, but  on DEC i have only 4 partitions a,b,g,h. A  graphics
> routine
> provided with the system fails to change the size of a partition when it
> is
>  in use or destroys all its files if the partition is not in use and I
> found little help on
> man pages.

Well, first of all, DEC's partitioning scheme. You have 8 partitions in
a label (a,b,c,d,e,f,g,h). Each one of them can have any offset and
size, as long as it is within disk boundaries. Consequently, partitions
may overlap.

So, what's the idea of overlaping partitions, when you can't use them at
the same time? The answer is that those are usually default labels and
they are designed to be multipurpose. If want the whole disk, use "c".
If it is a system disk, then use "a"-root, "b"-swap, "g"-usr, "h"-var.

Of course, this is, usually, not to your liking. So, when you are ready
to install the system you should make your custom disk label and have
the partition layout you want.

----------------

In your case, I'd say you've used up your disk space, "abgh" scheme
covers the whole disk. So, unless you would like to backup your machine
and re-arange your disk, you have only one solution. Get another disk in
and make secondary swap on one (or more) partitions on that disk.

Nix.



