Knut =?iso-8859-1?Q?Helleb=F8?=  <Knut.Hellebo@nho.hydro.com> wrote:
:Can any of you help me out with this one: A user logged on an OCTANE runn=
:ing
:6.5 issues a 'ls' on a autofs mounted (NFS3) directory from a DEC Alpha N=
:FS
:server. The response is nada. No files are listed (yes, it is confirmed t=
:hat
:the directory really contains some files by doing 'ls' on the server
:itself). When the user issues a 'touch' on the directory followed by a 'l=
:s'
:everything is OK, ie all the files are 'visible' again. The server runs
:Digital Unix 4.0b, patchset 5, Oct. 97.

You'll want to define 'nada' more clearly.  If the response were truly
'nada', then the SGI would think the DEC machine is down.  :)  So the
DEC is responding, but not with something the SGI likes.  There are a
couple basic possibilities:

1) The DEC is saying something right and the SGI is doing something wrong.
2) The DEC is saying something wrong and the SGI is doing what it's told.

I'd suggest running snoop and seeing what the conversation between the
SGI and the DEC looks like.  I'd also suggest mounting by hand testing
with some different mount options -- NFS over TCP, NFS2, noac, and see
if there's some sort of common theme.

